            <ArticleTitle>Successful treatment of intractable complex regional pain syndrome type I of the knee with dorsal root ganglion stimulation: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>58-60; discussion 60-1</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/ner.12190</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report on the efficacy of dorsal root ganglion stimulation in a patient with complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS) type I of the knee.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 48-year-old woman with CRPS type I of the right knee, diagnosed according to the Budapest criteria set, received DRG stimulation for intractable CRPS type I of the knee.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">After a successful trial period with three DRG stimulation leads on spinal levels L2, L3, and L4 (covering 90% of the painful area of her knee), a definitive pulse generator was implanted. Three months after implantation, the entire painful area was covered, and the patient reported a numeric rating scale score of 1-2.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Placement of three DRG stimulation leads at levels L2, L3, and L4 in a patient with intractable CRPS type I of the knee resulted in major pain relief. We recommend further investigation of the effect of DRG stimulation on pain due to CRPS of the knee.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2014 International Neuromodulation Society.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
